+# Code quality checks
+
+## Gate
+
+Use this when you have changed code and are about to say the work is done. Skip it for edits that only touch docs or comments.
+
+## Contract
+
+1. **Run the project's tests, not just the ones you think matter.** Use the project's own test command (`<TEST_CMD>`: fill this in, e.g. `npm test` or `pytest -q`). Report the real pass/fail output. If a test fails, say so; don't describe the work as finished.
+2. **Add or update a test for the behaviour you changed.** It should fail without your change and pass with it. If a test isn't practical, say why.
+3. **List the edge cases for each input you touched, then check each one.** Write down which of these apply and how each is handled (by a test or by the code): empty, missing or null; zero, negative, maximum or off-by-one boundaries; duplicates; non-ASCII text; very large input; errors from I/O or the network. Don't add handling for cases that cannot happen.
+4. **Read your own diff before finishing.** Remove debug output, dead code and unrelated changes. Follow the style of the code around it and of the project's linter (`<LINT_CMD>`).
+
+## Before you finish
+
+- The test command ran in this session, and its result is quoted, failures included.
+- Each edge case on the list is either covered by a test or has a one-line reason it was left out.
+- The diff contains only changes that belong to this task.
